#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fa65a2c-7495-580a-9acc-6e12591e327b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282963} {:answer/id #uuid "c08c4809-ddd8-5211-acb1-2f626299cd14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282964} {:answer/id #uuid "7003be15-2da1-5dd8-bb92-a36d2643349e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(-\\sqrt{3})$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282965} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffef04f-42d1-56b8-9198-1dea1a53ed3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282966} {:answer/id #uuid "9b2c5b8c-a2b4-5ec1-aa3c-ca0fbb8e00d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c08c4809-ddd8-5211-acb1-2f626299cd14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282964} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f2313a8-c044-5d0e-bf6b-5065dd5449ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d39d4010-b0be-5ef1-9cec-21d22a7b88ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-104148" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}, & x<-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3, & x=-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}, & x>-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- $f(-\\sqrt{3})=3,$ so $f(-\\sqrt{3})$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+3}{3} \\\\  & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})^{+}}(\\frac{12}{{x}^{2}+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)=2.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)=2,$ while $f(-\\sqrt{3})=3.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-\\sqrt{3})}f(x)\\ne f(-\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0d614d0-b4c9-5312-a663-abdd8b0fc55e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282970} {:answer/id #uuid "2b00a938-df83-5304-b348-a337b474bc3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282971} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c95a83-2e23-5610-96ca-3ba1c9ebd916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282972} {:answer/id #uuid "fe414019-f13f-56b9-ad5e-e911031342d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282973} {:answer/id #uuid "feba31f9-6a3a-58d6-abf8-8fdc6ee943ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7c95a83-2e23-5610-96ca-3ba1c9ebd916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282972} :answer-field/id #uuid "34e8146f-d5ef-5dd8-a4ef-ca02d25ebb95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c90ff895-7a2f-5133-9a57-0160b2c4493e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104153" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=5$ and $f(5)=2,$ then what is the value of\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-25f(x)}\\,?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=5.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5}f(x)=f(5)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5}\\sqrt{{x}^{3}-25f(x)} & =\\sqrt{{5}^{3}-25\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd3ea07-cbff-5758-9170-a2341e3862bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282977} {:answer/id #uuid "f7626db6-d91b-595b-97e3-8701e4013221" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282978} {:answer/id #uuid "f9117642-6733-53f7-bf3b-9c89f62e58b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282979} {:answer/id #uuid "b1877fb0-9ef0-5952-ad53-72dcf11cc56f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282980} {:answer/id #uuid "2b439bc1-88d9-58df-a770-f6057ccf23eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b439bc1-88d9-58df-a770-f6057ccf23eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282981} :answer-field/id #uuid "084bd079-7d65-5288-a595-ab51054fe31b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd3ee4e1-bde0-55c3-ada1-52860b4310e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104155" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ and $f(4)=-6,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x}?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=4.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)$ exists, and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=f(4)=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}\\frac{(f(x))^{2}}{6x} & =\\frac{(-6)^{2}}{6\\cdot 4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70029065-69c3-585d-a6a1-cc9d2485a60a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Continuity to Evaluate a Limit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "574b66c1-94ac-5fe7-acf1-4a43bd825538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282984} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee13174-d39f-55c0-904d-1fe1bd2c76c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282985} {:answer/id #uuid "cfea97c7-d075-5226-a29d-108eccd3daa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282986} {:answer/id #uuid "1f9c48ab-d01d-56d9-87a5-2c509236d7ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282987} {:answer/id #uuid "dfba1d7a-f9fc-5c58-b59d-be0cfac8da1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f9c48ab-d01d-56d9-87a5-2c509236d7ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282987} :answer-field/id #uuid "4013e0db-44fd-5103-8b3c-d13054790e83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b285893-8446-5b57-9a9f-346942f2893b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37900" :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=-3,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=f(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa1ddb40-3494-541c-b23e-b15dd98bf669" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reason for a Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f19a510-9dda-5f8a-8662-fd8d75f1f3cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ is a piecewise function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282991} {:answer/id #uuid "a8313eb5-4a7a-56c9-a3b6-7b948beef0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282992} {:answer/id #uuid "636dc83b-2078-556f-bda2-0c06efe05c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282993} {:answer/id #uuid "83955748-13e1-574c-9374-a277ae228439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(1)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282994} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b2c3f4-78d9-5140-a3da-258fd8f2ccb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8313eb5-4a7a-56c9-a3b6-7b948beef0ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282992} :answer-field/id #uuid "6eec9ca3-e37d-561b-aafd-39b1cb8e7283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a80d358-5059-5e32-b5f6-cc17b63eac94" :question/math-academy-id "q-48106" :question/problem "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ -x, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1.$\n\n- $f(1)=1,$ so $f(1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(x) \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(-x) \\\\  & =-(1) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).$"}]}
